Ladybird records the tile of a repeating SVG background at the scale
that the transforms of its box accumulate to, so the tile’s raster has
the size that the tile has on the screen, to within a pixel. It chooses
that scale when it records the tile, as it does for its other SVG
images: When a transform changes without the box being painted again,
the tile keeps the scale it was recorded at.

The other engines choose the scale when they rasterize, going by their
sources. In Blink, SVGImage::DrawPatternForContainer records the tile
into a paint-record shader, which PaintShader::CreateScaledPaintRecord
then rasterizes at the scale of the matrix it’s drawn under. WebKit’s
SVGImage::drawPattern sizes its image buffer by the xScale() and
yScale() of the context’s matrix. Gecko’s VectorImage::Draw bypasses its
surface cache when it draws into a recording, so WebRender rasterizes
the drawing commands themselves.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 (the build
that Playwright ships), a 24px SVG background stays sharp under
scale(4), scale(2, 3), scale(4.3), scale(40), a rotation with scale(3),
and scale(4) translateZ(1px) — repeating or not, animated or not, with a
viewBox or without one, and with a background-blend-mode. Ladybird now
matches Chrome in each of those cases.

Ladybird renders the image at one scale in both axes, in a viewport of
the size of the tile’s destination. So under scale(2, 3), an image whose
viewBox is fitted to its viewport keeps its layout, and its circle
becomes an ellipse, as it does in Chrome.

Ladybird limits the tile to 2048x2048 pixels, and to 8192 along a side.
Blink’s PaintShader::GetClampedRasterizationTileRect limits its tile to
the largest texture. So under scale(1000), Ladybird and Chrome both show
the edge of the tile’s curve soft, where WebKit shows it sharp. (Firefox
never finished its screenshot of that case.)

Where the scale would give the tile a fractional size in device pixels,
Ladybird lowers it to the nearest scale that gives the tile whole pixels
on each side. The tile is then drawn a little larger than it was
rasterized, which softens it: Under scale(3.7), a 24x12 tile is recorded
at 88x44, and comes out softer in Ladybird than in Chrome.

One difference is left as it was. All three engines draw an SVG
background with “image-rendering: pixelated” as smooth under scale(4) as
they draw one without it. Ladybird keeps such a tile at the size of its
destination, so it shows the pixels of that tile enlarged.
